.TH LORE 1 "2026-11-23" "lore 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lore \- every command you can type, and what each one does, offline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lore
.br
.B lore
.I NAME
.br
.B lore
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lore
lists every command you can type, each name once, sorted A to Z under
section letters (names that start with a digit or a symbol go under
.BR # ).
It needs no internet; with one, each page adds what cheat.sh has for the
command (see
.BR INTERNET ).
.PP
The list holds the programs on your
.B $PATH
and in the places a
.B $PATH
often leaves out (/usr/sbin, Homebrew, MacPorts, Nix, Snap, Flatpak,
~/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, ~/go/bin, /opt/*/bin and, on macOS, the directories
in /etc/paths), the builtins and keywords of your shell (bash, zsh or fish,
from
.BR $SHELL ),
and your aliases, each shown with what it runs.
.PP
A command's page shows, top to bottom: what it is and does; VERSION, its
package, the installed and the newest known version and how to update it,
read offline from the package manager (dpkg and apt, rpm, pacman or
Homebrew's folders); HOW TO USE, its most common uses; NOTES; FILES, the
configuration, logs and data its manual names; LINKS, to its
own site and to Stack Overflow, GeeksforGeeks and Google searches; and its
manual page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I NAME
Open
.IR NAME 's
page straight away, as
.B man
.I NAME
would. Everything works from there as usual: Left or Esc go back to
the list, and Esc there quits. A name not found on this machine still
opens if lore or
.B man
has a page for it.
.TP
.I "PROGRAM SUBCOMMAND"
Open a subcommand's page, such as
.BR "lore git commit" .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-print " " \fINAME\fR
Write
.IR NAME 's
page to standard output as plain text, with the same sections, and quit;
for pipes and scripts
.RB ( "lore --print tar | less" ).
It is laid out at the terminal's width, or 80 columns in a pipe or a file,
and needs no terminal.
.TP
.BI "explain " LINE
Each word of a command line and what it means, offline:
.B lore explain """tar -xzvf a.tgz"""
gives tar's summary, then each flag
.RB ( \-x ,
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-f )
with the first sentence its man page gives it, and
.I a.tgz
as the value of
.BR \-f .
Joined short flags are split, a long option's
.I =value
is read, a pipe or a list
.RB ( | ,
.BR && ,
.BR ; )
explains every command in it, redirections say where the output goes, and
.BR sudo ,
.B env
or
.B xargs
are explained, then the command they run. Without quotes the words are
taken as they come (the shell may change some).
.TP
.BI "\-\-completions " SHELL
Print the completion script for
.BR bash ,
.B zsh
or
.BR fish .
The packages install them already. Otherwise: in ~/.bashrc,
.BR "source <(lore --completions bash)" ;
for zsh,
.B lore --completions zsh > ~/.zfunc/_lore
with ~/.zfunc on
.BR $fpath ;
for fish,
.BR "lore --completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/lore.fish" .
Then Tab completes names
.RB ( "lore gi" ),
a program's subcommands
.RB ( "lore git " ),
recipes word by word, the options, and after
.B explain
a program's flags from its manual; zsh and fish show what each one is.
.TP
.BI "check " FILE...
Check page files, for a program that ships its own page
.RI ( NAME.md ).
Errors keep a page from being shown, and make
.B lore check
exit with 1: a title that is not
.IR NAME ,
control characters or escape sequences, invisible characters that hide or
reorder text, HTML or script, an example that runs what it downloads
.RB ( "curl ... | sh" ),
more than 64 KB, or text that is not UTF-8. Warnings: risky examples
(marked
.BR ! ),
links that are not https, have a user or a password, a punycode or a bare
address for a host, or are too long to show whole.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage text.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print lore's version.
.SH KEYS
.SS In the list
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through the commands; the letter headers are skipped.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Switch the topic, in the bar under the search bar: commands, recipes,
most used, recent, bookmarks (see
.BR TOPICS ).
Round at either end.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the selected command's page.
.TP
.B /
Go to the search bar. Typing keeps the names that contain the text,
ignoring case, ranked best first instead of A to Z: the exact name, then
names starting with the text (shorter first), then names with it at the
start of a word (git rm), then anywhere. From three characters on, the
pages are searched too, by their categories, summary and examples, and
their matches follow: "compress folder" lists tar and zip, and a command
named by one of the words ("cut files") comes first among them. Words
match at the start of words ("cut" finds "cuts", not "execute"), and a
plural also finds its singular. Each result shows, after its name, the
page line that matched, cut around the match
.RB ( "[Cut out fields from stdin or files.]" ),
then its categories. With the search empty, the list is A to Z again. Common words count as their usual equivalents
(folder as directory, delete as remove). Start
with
.B @
to keep only the commands that have a lore page
.RB ( @gr ).
Esc leaves the search bar and keeps its text; Backspace erases it.
.TP
.B b
Bookmark the selected command, or take its bookmark away (see
.BR "YOUR OWN USE" ).
.TP
.B Tab, Shift+Tab
Jump to the next or the previous letter: its first command, its header on
top. Round at either end: A, B, C, ... and A again.
.TP
.B +
About lore: its version, what it found on this machine (commands, pages,
translations), where it keeps its settings and lists, its licence and
source; and the settings, changed there: Colours (Enter opens the palette
picker), Internet (on or off) and History (read or not). Up and Down choose
one, Enter changes it, and it is saved at once for the next runs. +, Left or
Esc go back; p opens the palette picker too.
.TP
.B Esc
Quit.
.SS On a page
.TP
.B Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn
Scroll.
.TP
.B /
Search other commands without leaving the page: the list beside it
filters as you type, and Enter or Right opens the chosen command's page in
place. Esc leaves the search for the full list.
.TP
.B Left, Esc
Back to the list, or quit when the page was opened as
.B lore
.IR NAME .
.TP
.B c
Copy one of the page's examples: a list of them opens over the page, Up and
Down choose, Enter copies (the key bar says what), Esc closes. lore asks
the terminal to copy it (the OSC 52 escape), so it works over ssh and with
no clipboard tool installed; most terminals do it, tmux with
.BR "set-clipboard on" ,
and one that does not simply ignores it.
.TP
.B b
Bookmark this page's command, or take its bookmark away; a bookmarked
page's title starts with \(**.
.TP
.B Tab, Shift+Tab
Jump to the next or the previous section (VERSION, HOW TO USE, RELATED,
\&...), round at either end.
.SS In the palette picker
.TP
.B Up, Down
Try the palette above or below on the whole screen.
.TP
.B Enter
Keep it, and save it for the next runs.
.TP
.B Esc
Go back to the palette from before.
.SS Anywhere
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Quit.
.SH CATEGORIES AND DANGER
In the list, each command is followed by its categories, such as
.BR "[archives files]" .
.B danger
marks a command that can destroy data or stop the system; its page warns
under the summary, and every example that can is marked with
.BR ! .
.SH LANGUAGES
Pages are shown in the language of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.B LC_MESSAGES
or
.B LANG
when lore has a translation (from tldr-pages, 41 languages); otherwise in
English.
.SH TOPICS
Each kind of thing lore knows is a topic, with a list of its own; the bar
under the search bar shows them, and Left and Right switch between them.
.B commands
is every command on this machine, A to Z;
.B recipes
the task pages (see
.BR RECIPES );
.BR "most used" ,
.B recent
and
.B bookmarks
your own (see
.BR "YOUR OWN USE" ).
A topic with nothing in it yet is not shown. A search looks in every topic.
.SH RECIPES
The
.B recipes
topic holds 100 short task pages that chain commands: free disk space, what is
using a port, why won't a service start, add a user, find files containing
a text, copy files to and from a server, read a file line by line, and
more, most of them the terminal tasks people ask about most. Each shows numbered STEPS (risky ones
marked), the commands it USES, and notes for other distributions and
macOS. The search finds them by name and by what they say;
.B c
copies a step's command. They are in lore's repository, recipes/, one
Markdown file each.
.SH YOUR OWN USE
Three topics are yours:
.B bookmarks
(added and removed with
.BR b ),
.B recent
(the last 10 pages opened) and
.B most used
(the 50 commands your shell history ran most, most first). In a search, among names that match the same way,
the ones you run most come first. A dim dot after a name marks a command
your history never ran.
.PP
The history is read from ~/.bash_history, ~/.zsh_history, ~/.histfile,
~/.local/share/fish/fish_history and
.BR $HISTFILE ,
whichever exist. lore only reads it, keeps nothing from it, and sends
nothing anywhere;
.I "history = off"
in the settings file (or History on the + screen) stops it reading it.
.SH COLOURS
On the + screen, Enter on Colours (or
.BR p )
opens the palette picker; Enter saves it in the settings file (see
.BR FILES ).
.B LORE_PALETTE
names one for a single run, over the saved one.
.PP
Built in:
.B terminal
(the default, the terminal's own colours), Catppuccin, Dracula, Nord,
Gruvbox, Solarized, Tokyo Night, One and Ros\('e Pine, most in a dark and a
light variant. Every palette but
.B terminal
paints its own background. An unknown name gives the default; case does
not matter.
.PP
Your own: a file
.I NAME.palette
in
.I ~/.config/lore/palettes/
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lore/palettes/ ),
read at start; one with a built-in palette's name takes its place, and one
that does not read is skipped. One
.I "key = value"
a line:
.BR background ,
.BR text ,
.BR muted ,
.BR selection ,
.BR primary ,
.BR secondary ,
.BR accent ,
.BR danger ,
.B code
and
.BR warning ,
each
.I #rrggbb
or a terminal colour name
.RB ( cyan ,
.BR reset ...).
The palettes/ folder of lore's repository has the built-in ones and the
format in full.
.SH INTERNET
A dot on the search bar,
.BR "\(bu internet" ,
is green when cht.sh can be reached and red when not; the about screen
.RB ( + )
says the same. A command's page then has an INTERNET section before
MANUAL, with the dot, and that command's community cheat sheet from
.IR https://cht.sh/NAME ,
fetched in the background when the page opens and kept for a day in
~/.cache/lore/internet/.
.PP
It is raw text, written by anyone and not checked: it can be changed at
any time. lore takes out every control and escape character, shows it as
it came behind a bar, never turns it into links, never copies it
.RB ( c
copies only the page's own examples) and never runs it. Read it, and copy
from it with care.
.PP
.I "internet = off"
in the settings file, or Internet turned off on the + screen: lore makes
no request at all, and the dot is red.
.SH LINKS
Every URL is shown whole, on a line of its own, so the terminal can open
it: Ctrl+click in Konsole and most terminals. lore never opens a link
itself, shows only https links, and never asks you to log in anywhere or
for any account. Check a link before you open it: anyone can add pages.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lore/config
.TQ
.I ~/.config/lore/config
lore's settings, written when a setting is changed in the app (the +
screen, or p for colours): one
.I "key = value"
a line,
.B #
starts a comment:
.IR "palette = NAME" ,
.I "history = off"
and
.IR "internet = off" .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/lore/internet/NAME.txt
cht.sh's answer for
.IR NAME ,
kept for a day
.RB ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME ).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/lore/bookmarks
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/lore/bookmarks
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/lore/recent
Your bookmarks and recently opened pages, one name a line.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lore/content/NAME.md
.TQ
.I /usr/local/share/lore/content/NAME.md
.TQ
.I /usr/share/lore/content/NAME.md
A page for
.IR NAME ,
and its translations in
.IR i18n/LANG/NAME.md
under the same folders. Yours, in ~/.local/share/lore/content, come first
and may replace a page built into lore. The other two are where programs
install a page for their own command: lore uses them only for a command it
has no page for, so no package can rewrite another command's page. Every
one must pass
.B lore check
or it is not shown. None of these folders needs to exist. The format is in
content/README.md in lore's repository.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal quit; 1 when there is no terminal to draw on (a pipe, or
.B ssh
without
.BR \-t ),
on an unknown option, or when
.I NAME
has no command, lore page or manual page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR whatis (1),
.BR apropos (1)
.PP
https://github.com/guhhammer/lore
